+## Phase 5 — Preprocessing
+
+`src/agentsentinel/data/preprocess.py` loads Phase 2 synthetic traces
+(`data/synthetic/`) and Phase 4 collected traces (`data/collected/`),
+flattens each trace's nested events into one row per event, and writes a
+versioned Parquet table plus a manifest under `data/processed/<version>/`.
+
+Why preprocessing is separate from feature engineering:
+
+- Phase 5 only cleans and reshapes what the Phase 3 schema already
+  captures: nested fields to flat columns, timestamp parsing, type
+  coercion, and deduplication. It does not compute anything derived
+  (rates, ratios, rolling windows, baselines) -- that is Phase 6.
+  Mixing the two would make it impossible to re-run feature engineering
+  against a differently-shaped feature set without also re-deciding
+  cleaning/typing rules, and would make Phase 5's output harder to trust
+  as a stable contract for both Phase 6 and Phase 7.
+- Row granularity is one row per tool-call event, with trace-level fields
+  (trace_id, label, anomaly_type, data_type, agent_id, task_id, task_type,
+  dataset_version, limitations, policy_version) repeated onto every event
+  row belonging to that trace. Per-event operational fields (latency_ms,
+  token_usage, retry_count, status) are what Phase 6/7 need directly; a
+  nested list-of-events column would just force every downstream consumer
+  to re-flatten it.
+- Tool arguments (`tool_input_metadata`) are kept as a JSON string column,
+  not expanded into per-key columns. Argument shape varies by tool_name
+  (search_customer/get_order/update_order each have different keys), so a
+  fixed set of flat columns cannot represent them without inventing a
+  per-tool schema -- that belongs to Phase 6, not Phase 5.
+
+Provenance: a `source` column records which pipeline produced each row
+(`"synthetic"` vs `"collected"`), independent of the trace's own
+`data_type` field (`"synthetic"`/`"real"`). The two are never merged or
+derived from one another -- a real production trace captured by the Phase 4
+collector still has `source="collected"`, `data_type="real"`, and that
+combination is itself useful signal, per the "do not silently merge
+dataset types" rule.
+
+Null-handling choices:
+
+- `label` (null on real/unlabeled traces) -> sentinel `"unlabeled"`.
+- `anomaly_type` (null on normal/unlabeled traces) -> sentinel `"none"`.
+- `policy_version` (null until the policy engine phase exists) -> sentinel
+  `"unassigned"`.
+- Rows are never dropped for a missing label/anomaly_type/policy_version.
+  An unlabeled real trace is still a valid row for Phase 6/7 -- it just
+  cannot be used as a supervised-learning label. Explicit string sentinels
+  were used instead of leaving `None`/`NaN` so that groupby/value_counts/
+  one-hot operations in later phases see one consistent dtype per column
+  instead of a silent mix of `str` and `float('nan')`.
+
+Type coercion: `retry_count`, `latency_ms`, `token_usage`,
+`output_record_count`, and `output_result_size_bytes` are coerced to
+pandas' nullable `Int64` (not numpy `int64`, which has no way to represent
+a missing value); `estimated_cost_usd` to `float64`; `event_timestamp` is
+parsed from the raw ISO string to a UTC-aware `datetime64` column (the raw
+string column is dropped once parsed, to avoid two representations of the
+same fact).
+
+Deduplication: rows are deduplicated by the composite key
+`(trace_id, event_id)`, not `event_id` alone. Per the Phase 3 schema,
+`event_id` should already be globally unique, but the composite costs
+nothing extra and does not rely on that uniqueness assumption holding
+against real (not just synthetic) collected data, which Phase 5 has not
+yet been exercised against at volume.
+
+Rejection-log exclusion: `data/collected/rejected/rejections.jsonl` is
+explicitly excluded from loading (`discover_trace_files` skips any path
+under a `rejected/` segment). That file holds malformed, non-Trace-shaped
+payloads written by `TraceCollector._reject()` -- it is diagnostic output
+about invalid input, not trace data, and must never be silently ingested
+as if it were.
+
+Versioned output format:
+
+- Parquet, not CSV, chosen because this dataset is read repeatedly by
+  Phase 6 and Phase 7, and Parquet preserves exact dtypes (nullable Int64,
+  UTC-aware datetime64, float64) round-trip; CSV would silently degrade
+  every one of those on load unless every consumer re-declares dtypes by
+  hand. The tradeoff is one added dependency, `pyarrow`, now declared in
+  `pyproject.toml` alongside a new `pandas` dependency (this project had no
